{
  "scenes": {
    "apply_jobs": {
      "description": "Run a browser-assisted application workflow for one approved freelance lead. Example triggers: 'apply to the approved freelance lead', 'bewerbe mich auf den freigegebenen Auftrag', 'submit the application form for this project', 'Bewerbung für den genehmigten Lead einreichen'.",
      "task": "Use web_task_coordinator to review one approved lead, gather any missing context, and delegate browser submission steps only when stored credentials exist for the target site and secure credential fill has been approved.",
      "webhookKey": "$SCENE_APPLY_JOBS_KEY",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "web_task_coordinator",
        "browser_agent",
        "researcher",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "humanInLoopSteps": [
        "site_fill_credentials",
        "mcp__playwright__browser_navigate"
      ],
      "approvalChannel": "slack-approvals",
      "params": {
        "minRating": {
          "description": "Minimum lead rating to accept",
          "default": "0.7"
        }
      }
    },
    "browser_inspection": {
      "description": "Open a website, capture Playwright browser evidence, and summarize what is visible or blocked on the page. Example triggers: 'check what's currently shown on example.com', 'prüfe welche Inhalte auf der Webseite sichtbar sind', 'inspect the landing page and report visible text', 'schaue dir die Seite an und beschreibe was zu sehen ist', 'verify the page loaded correctly after login', 'Browser-Snapshot mit Zusammenfassung erstellen'.",
      "task": "Use web_task_coordinator first when the page may be JS-rendered, multi-step, or ambiguous. Delegate browser_agent to open the target website, wait for the relevant page state, and capture browser_snapshot plus browser_screenshot evidence when the rendered view matters. Use vision_browser_analyst or evidence_analyst to interpret the captured page evidence, then summarizer to produce a concise grounded inspection result with exact visible text, URLs, blockers, and next steps.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "web_task_coordinator",
        "browser_agent",
        "vision_browser_analyst",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "humanInLoopSteps": [
        "site_fill_credentials"
      ],
      "params": {
        "targetUrl": {
          "description": "Website or page URL to open and inspect",
          "default": ""
        },
        "inspectionGoal": {
          "description": "What should be checked, extracted, or verified on the page",
          "default": "capture a snapshot and screenshot of the relevant page state, then summarize what is visible"
        },
        "captureMode": {
          "description": "Preferred evidence capture mode",
          "default": "snapshot plus screenshot when the rendered layout matters"
        }
      }
    },
    "n8n_project_list": {
      "description": "Open the n8n login page, fill the stored site credentials, navigate to the project-list URL from the site's named URLs, and read the visible table or Projektliste to check for new entries. Stop after listing the workflows and ask whether one of the found workflows should be run.",
      "task": "Use browser_agent to execute this exact browser flow and nothing broader. Do NOT use web_search. Call get_site_credentials for the hostname from {{targetUrl}} to read the loginUrl, named URLs, selectors, and notes. Open the loginUrl from the site credentials when present; otherwise open {{targetUrl}}. Take a browser_snapshot to identify the login form fields, then call site_fill_credentials to fill and submit the stored credentials securely. Do NOT wait for generic dashboard text such as Projects, Home, Dashboard, or other landing-page labels before proceeding. Do NOT wait for or re-check login-form text such as Sign in, Email, or Password, and do NOT click the submit button again. If the post-login state is ambiguous, use a short fixed delay and then continue to the named URL. Immediately inspect the named URLs from get_site_credentials and pick the entry whose label best matches {{projectListUrlHint}}. If no matching named URL exists, stop and report that the site credentials entry is missing the project-list URL instead of guessing a path. Navigate directly to that named URL after the secure credential submit, even if the post-login landing page has not visibly changed yet. Stay on the same n8n.k2o host from the stored credentials and do not guess localhost, app.n8n.io, n8n.io, or other alternate hosts. Wait up to {{navigationTimeout}} seconds for the final project-list page to load, take a browser_snapshot, and read the visible table rows directly. Prefer browser_snapshot over browser_screenshot for table extraction, and answer immediately once the visible rows are readable. Return {{outputFormat}} with the final URL, the visible table contents, whether any clearly new entries are visible, and any blocker encountered. IMPORTANT: Do not start or click any n8n workflow from this scene. This scene ends at the project list only. If exactly one workflow clearly stands out as the new or relevant entry, end with a direct question asking whether that workflow should be run and include a final line `RUN_CANDIDATE: <workflow name>`. If multiple plausible workflows are visible, ask which listed workflow should be run next and do not emit a RUN_CANDIDATE line. If no workflow should be run, say so clearly. Do not delegate to web_task_coordinator, vision_browser_analyst, evidence_analyst, or summarizer for this workflow.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "browser_agent"
      ],
      "humanInLoopSteps": [
        "site_fill_credentials",
        "mcp__playwright__browser_navigate"
      ],
      "approvalChannel": "slack-approvals",
      "params": {
        "targetUrl": {
          "description": "Initial URL to open (supports internal HTTP domains like http://n8n.k2o)",
          "default": "http://n8n.k2o"
        },
        "projectListUrlHint": {
          "description": "Preferred named URL labels in site credentials for the project-list destination",
          "default": "project-list, project-list-url, projects, projektliste"
        },
        "outputFormat": {
          "description": "Report format for the table readout",
          "default": "a concise markdown report"
        },
        "navigationTimeout": {
          "description": "Navigation timeout in seconds for page loads and the final table read",
          "default": "120"
        }
      }
    },
    "n8n_run_workflow": {
      "description": "After the user confirms a listed n8n workflow, open n8n, log in with stored credentials, find the named workflow in the project list, and start that one workflow.",
      "task": "Use browser_agent to execute this exact browser flow and nothing broader. Do NOT use web_search. Call get_site_credentials for the hostname from {{targetUrl}} to read the loginUrl, named URLs, selectors, and notes. Open the loginUrl from the site credentials when present; otherwise open {{targetUrl}}. Take a browser_snapshot to identify the login form fields, then call site_fill_credentials to fill and submit the stored credentials securely. Do NOT wait for generic dashboard text such as Projects, Home, Dashboard, or other landing-page labels before proceeding. Do NOT wait for or re-check login-form text such as Sign in, Email, or Password, and do NOT click the submit button again. If the post-login state is ambiguous, use a short fixed delay and then continue to the named URL. Inspect the named URLs from get_site_credentials and pick the entry whose label best matches {{projectListUrlHint}}. If no matching named URL exists, stop and report that the site credentials entry is missing the project-list URL instead of guessing a path. Navigate directly to that named URL after the secure credential submit, even if the post-login landing page has not visibly changed yet. Stay on the same n8n.k2o host from the stored credentials and do not guess localhost, app.n8n.io, n8n.io, or other alternate hosts. Wait up to {{navigationTimeout}} seconds for the table to load. Take a browser_snapshot and locate the row whose visible workflow name best matches {{workflowName}}. Prefer browser_snapshot over browser_screenshot, and if no exact or unambiguous match is visible, stop and report the visible candidate workflow names instead of guessing. Open only that workflow entry, take another browser_snapshot, identify the primary run or execute control, click it once, and wait for a visible confirmation that execution has started. Return {{outputFormat}} with the matched workflow name, the final URL, the visible execution status, and any blocker encountered. Do not start any additional workflows.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "browser_agent"
      ],
      "humanInLoopSteps": [
        "site_fill_credentials",
        "mcp__playwright__browser_navigate"
      ],
      "approvalChannel": "slack-approvals",
      "params": {
        "targetUrl": {
          "description": "Initial URL to open (supports internal HTTP domains like http://n8n.k2o)",
          "default": "http://n8n.k2o"
        },
        "projectListUrlHint": {
          "description": "Preferred named URL labels in site credentials for the project-list destination",
          "default": "project-list, project-list-url, projects, projektliste"
        },
        "workflowName": {
          "description": "Exact or near-exact workflow name that the user confirmed should be started",
          "default": "the workflow the user just confirmed"
        },
        "outputFormat": {
          "description": "Report format for the workflow start result",
          "default": "a concise markdown report"
        },
        "navigationTimeout": {
          "description": "Navigation timeout in seconds for page loads and workflow start confirmation",
          "default": "120"
        }
      }
    },
    "source_backed_paper": {
      "description": "Produce a paper, report, or research brief grounded in official sources and verified citations. Example triggers: 'write a research paper on quantum-safe cryptography', 'erstelle ein Forschungsdossier zum Thema KI-Regulierung', 'produce a cited technical brief', 'verfasse einen quellenbasierten Report', 'research and write up the GDPR compliance overview', 'Forschungsbericht mit Quellennachweisen schreiben'.",
      "task": "When attachments or prior reports are provided, start with document_intake. Use researcher for source discovery — it handles both broad web research and citation-grade official sources. Use evidence_analyst to reconcile findings into a clean evidence ledger, and research_librarian to persist durable notes and graph links when the evidence base is large. For short briefs, use summarizer to produce the final deliverable. For full papers or reports, draft only from collected evidence with paper_author. Use source_verifier only to validate citations or claim-level gaps. Run exactly one final quality pass with quality_supervisor before finalizing. Do not create an ephemeral writer and do not rerun the whole pipeline after a viable draft already exists unless the quality pass names one smallest corrective action. Do not delegate to mission_coordinator from this scene — the research pipeline is already defined here.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "document_intake",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "research_librarian",
        "paper_author",
        "source_verifier",
        "researcher",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard",
        "quality_supervisor"
      ],
      "params": {
        "outputStyle": {
          "description": "Requested style or citation format",
          "default": "technical report with inline references"
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience",
          "default": "technical stakeholder"
        },
        "freshnessRequirement": {
          "description": "How fresh the evidence needs to be",
          "default": "use the freshest reliable sources available and call out publication dates"
        }
      }
    },
    "deep_research": {
      "description": "Run a multi-phase deep-research mission with evidence indexing, optional Mermaid visuals, and a final quality gate. Example triggers: 'conduct deep research on the battery supply chain', 'führe eine Tiefenrecherche zum Thema Energiewende durch', 'do a thorough investigation into this topic', 'umfassende Recherche mit Visualisierungen erstellen', 'full research dossier with evidence and timeline', 'Deep-Dive Recherche mit Qualitätsprüfung'.",
      "task": "Use mission_coordinator to partition the work into the smallest viable specialist graph. Use researcher for all source collection — it handles both broad web research and citation-grade official sources in a single agent. Use research_librarian to persist notes, graph links, and timelines. Use evidence_analyst to reconcile the ledger. Use data_analyst only when structured numbers matter. Use chart_designer for HTML charts or tables based on verified numeric evidence. Use diagram_designer when a structural visual model would clarify the answer. Use paper_author for the final deliverable when a full paper is requested, or summarizer for a short brief. Use quality_supervisor to decide whether one targeted corrective pass is justified before finalizing. IMPORTANT: mission_coordinator must NOT call search_workflows or run_workflow from inside this scene — the research pipeline is already defined. Delegate directly to the named specialists.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "mission_coordinator",
        "researcher",
        "research_librarian",
        "document_intake",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "data_analyst",
        "chart_designer",
        "diagram_designer",
        "paper_author",
        "summarizer",
        "quality_supervisor"
      ],
      "params": {
        "deliverable": {
          "description": "Requested final deliverable type",
          "default": "source-backed research dossier with explicit gaps and next actions"
        },
        "includeVisuals": {
          "description": "Whether Mermaid diagrams should be produced when they materially help",
          "default": "yes when a workflow, relationship map, architecture, or timeline would improve clarity"
        },
        "freshnessRequirement": {
          "description": "How recent the evidence must be",
          "default": "prefer the freshest reliable sources and preserve publication dates"
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience",
          "default": "technical stakeholder"
        },
        "visualFocus": {
          "description": "Preferred visual structure if one is appropriate",
          "default": "workflow, timeline, or relationship map depending on the evidence"
        }
      }
    },
    "meeting_briefing_packet": {
      "description": "Produce a concise meeting brief or handoff packet from attached material, prior findings, and project context. Example triggers: 'prepare a briefing for tomorrow's stakeholder meeting', 'erstelle ein Briefing für das Meeting mit dem Kunden', 'put together a handoff packet for the new PM', 'Übergabepaket für das Projektteam vorbereiten', 'summarize these notes into a meeting brief', 'Meeting-Brief mit Aktionspunkten und Risiken'.",
      "task": "Use document_intake first when source material includes files or long notes, data_analyst for tabular attachments, evidence_analyst to reconcile the evidence ledger, policy_compliance_reviewer when approvals or constraints matter, and meeting_briefing_agent to produce the final brief or handoff packet. Use qa_guard when the packet needs an explicit verification pass.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "document_intake",
        "data_analyst",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "policy_compliance_reviewer",
        "meeting_briefing_agent",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "audience": {
          "description": "Target audience for the packet",
          "default": "project stakeholders"
        },
        "outputFormat": {
          "description": "Preferred delivery format",
          "default": "markdown brief with actions, decisions, and open risks"
        }
      }
    },
    "code_review": {
      "description": "Git-based code review workflow: inspect changes, analyze quality, produce a structured review report, and optionally post the verdict as a PR comment on GitHub. Example triggers: 'review the latest commits on this branch', 'führe ein Code-Review der letzten Änderungen durch', 'analyze the diff for quality issues', 'prüfe den Pull Request auf Probleme', 'review HEAD~3..HEAD for bugs', 'Code-Review mit strukturiertem Bericht', 'review PR #42 and post a summary comment'.",
      "task": "Use git_developer to inspect the current diff and recent commits, code_analyst to review code quality and identify issues, qa_guard to verify the findings, and summarizer to produce the final review report. When a target branch or commit range is specified, use git_diff with the appropriate ref parameter. When the review is bound to an existing GitHub PR (param prNumber set), git_developer should also call github_pr_get to read the existing PR body for context, github_check_runs_list against the PR head SHA to fold CI status into the review, and github_pr_comment to post the final verdict on the PR thread once the operator approves it. Do not open a new PR from this scene — use software_bug_fix or code_refactor for that.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "git_developer",
        "code_analyst",
        "qa_guard",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "humanInLoopSteps": [
        "github_pr_comment"
      ],
      "approvalChannel": "slack-approvals",
      "params": {
        "targetRef": {
          "description": "Git ref, branch, or commit range to review",
          "default": "HEAD~5..HEAD"
        },
        "focusAreas": {
          "description": "Specific areas to focus on (security, performance, style, logic)",
          "default": "all"
        },
        "prNumber": {
          "description": "Optional GitHub PR number — when set, the review is folded into the PR (CI status fetched, verdict posted as a comment after approval)",
          "default": ""
        }
      }
    },
    "api_test_suite": {
      "description": "Test and validate API endpoints with structured request/response analysis and documentation. Example triggers: 'test the REST API endpoints of this service', 'teste die API-Endpunkte gegen die Spezifikation', 'validate the response schemas for /users and /orders', 'API-Test-Report für den neuen Service erstellen', 'run a smoke test suite against the staging API', 'API-Endpunkte systematisch prüfen'.",
      "task": "Use api_integrator to execute HTTP requests against the target API, researcher to gather API documentation if needed, code_analyst to review response schemas, and qa_guard to validate the test results. Produce a structured test report with pass/fail status, response details, and identified issues.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "api_integrator",
        "researcher",
        "code_analyst",
        "qa_guard",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "params": {
        "baseUrl": {
          "description": "Base URL of the API to test",
          "default": ""
        },
        "authMethod": {
          "description": "Authentication method (bearer, api-key, basic, none)",
          "default": "none"
        }
      }
    },
    "multi_channel_broadcast": {
      "description": "Draft and send a coordinated message across multiple communication channels with platform-appropriate formatting. Example triggers: 'announce the release on Slack, Discord, and email', 'verkünde die Wartung auf allen Kanälen', 'broadcast the incident status to the team channels', 'Nachricht an Telegram, Slack und E-Mail senden', 'coordinated announcement across Telegram and email', 'Multi-Channel-Nachricht mit angepasstem Format'.",
      "task": "Use summarizer or paper_author to draft the core message based on the user's input and any shared evidence, then notification_agent to send platform-adapted versions to the requested channels. Use qa_guard to review the draft before sending when accuracy is critical.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "notification_agent",
        "summarizer",
        "paper_author",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "channels": {
          "description": "Comma-separated list of target channels (telegram, slack, discord, email)",
          "default": "telegram"
        },
        "tone": {
          "description": "Message tone (formal, casual, urgent, informational)",
          "default": "informational"
        }
      }
    },
    "security_audit": {
      "description": "Research CVEs and security advisories for a specified stack or codebase, run static code analysis on high-risk paths, apply a compliance check, and produce a prioritised risk report. Example triggers: 'run a security audit on our Node.js dependencies', 'Sicherheits-Audit für das Docker-Deployment durchführen', 'check the code for security issues and CVE exposure', 'Sicherheitsüberprüfung mit CVE-Recherche und Compliance-Check', 'audit this repo for vulnerabilities', 'erstelle einen Sicherheitsbericht mit Risikotabelle'.",
      "task": "Use researcher to gather current CVE data, vendor advisories, and upstream security announcements for {{target}} at or above {{severityThreshold}}. Use code_analyst for static review of authentication, input handling, crypto, serialisation, and dependency-exposed code paths when the audit scope includes the source tree. Use policy_compliance_reviewer to map findings against the configured compliance requirements. Use evidence_analyst to reconcile CVE data with the code analysis into a single evidence ledger keyed by CVE id and affected file or dependency. Use summarizer to produce the final {{outputFormat}} with an ordered risk table (severity, CVE/finding, affected component, remediation, owner). Run one quality_supervisor pass before finalising. Do not attempt to patch vulnerabilities from this scene — the deliverable is the report; remediation is a separate task.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "researcher",
        "research_librarian",
        "code_analyst",
        "policy_compliance_reviewer",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard",
        "quality_supervisor"
      ],
      "params": {
        "target": {
          "description": "Software stack, repository path, service, or dependency set to audit",
          "default": "configured stack and npm dependencies"
        },
        "auditScope": {
          "description": "What to cover: dependencies, code, infrastructure, all",
          "default": "dependencies"
        },
        "severityThreshold": {
          "description": "Minimum CVSS severity to include (low, medium, high, critical)",
          "default": "medium"
        },
        "outputFormat": {
          "description": "Final report format",
          "default": "markdown with risk table and remediation plan"
        }
      }
    },
    "release_notes_draft": {
      "description": "Generate release notes from git history and diff analysis for a given ref range, formatted per Keep-a-Changelog conventions and tailored to the stated audience. Optionally publishes the notes as a GitHub Release. Example triggers: 'draft release notes for v2.3', 'Release Notes für den letzten Sprint erstellen', 'summarise the commits between HEAD~20 and HEAD', 'Versionshinweise aus Git-Log generieren', 'produce changelog entries for the upcoming release', 'technische Release-Notizen mit Breaking Changes', 'draft notes for v2.3 and publish the GitHub Release'.",
      "task": "Use git_developer to collect commits and diffs in {{targetRef}} and classify them into Added / Changed / Fixed / Removed / Security / Deprecated buckets (Keep-a-Changelog). Use code_analyst to flag breaking changes, migration-relevant diffs, and high-impact behaviour changes that the commit message alone may understate. Use paper_author to draft the notes for {{audience}} as grouped bullets with short, outcome-oriented phrasing — no commit hashes or author names unless the audience is `technical`. Use qa_guard to verify that no listed item contradicts the actual diff. Use summarizer to produce the final Keep-a-Changelog section headed by `## [{{version}}] — <ISO date>`. When {{publishGithubRelease}} is yes, git_developer should call github_release_create with tagName={{version}}, name={{version}}, body=the rendered notes, prerelease=true when the version contains '-rc' or '-beta', generateReleaseNotes=false (we just authored ours); this is approval-gated. Do not invent changes not visible in the diff.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "git_developer",
        "code_analyst",
        "paper_author",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "humanInLoopSteps": [
        "github_release_create"
      ],
      "approvalChannel": "slack-approvals",
      "params": {
        "version": {
          "description": "Release version label (e.g. v2.3, 0.7.0-rc1)",
          "default": "next"
        },
        "targetRef": {
          "description": "Git ref range to generate notes from",
          "default": "HEAD~20..HEAD"
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience: internal, public, technical",
          "default": "internal team"
        },
        "broadcast": {
          "description": "Whether to hand the notes to a broadcast step afterwards (yes/no)",
          "default": "no"
        },
        "publishGithubRelease": {
          "description": "Whether to publish the notes as a GitHub Release after the operator approves",
          "default": "no"
        }
      }
    },
    "incident_response": {
      "description": "Triage a live or recent production incident, gather diagnostic evidence from logs and services, identify root cause, guide or apply remediation, and capture findings for a postmortem. Example triggers: 'triage the database outage at 14:00', 'Vorfallsreaktion für den 500er Fehlerausbruch', 'investigate why payments are failing right now', 'Ausfall untersuchen und Ursache finden', 'run incident response on the gateway timeout', 'Incident-Response mit Ursachenanalyse und Behebung'.",
      "task": "Use ops_triage to direct the investigation. Run one or two targeted remote checks with shell_agent (ssh_exec, service_check) to confirm which of {{affectedService}} is failing and how. Use log_analyst to parse relevant logs around the incident window and build a concrete timeline with real timestamps, error codes, and affected requests. Use infrastructure_agent only when a clear, low-risk remediation is identified; for anything beyond service restart or cache flush, surface the proposed action and stop. Use evidence_analyst to consolidate the findings into a structured ledger: what failed, when, why (with quoted log lines), remediation taken (if any), and follow-up actions. Use summarizer to produce a concise status-update suitable for broadcast. Do not replace concrete stderr or log evidence with speculation — if the evidence is only an error phrase, return that exact phrase. Severity hint: {{severity}}.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "ops_triage",
        "shell_agent",
        "log_analyst",
        "infrastructure_agent",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "incident": {
          "description": "Description of the failure, alert, or symptom",
          "default": "the reported incident"
        },
        "severity": {
          "description": "Incident severity: low, medium, high, critical",
          "default": "high"
        },
        "affectedService": {
          "description": "Service, container, or host showing the failure",
          "default": "discover from logs"
        }
      }
    },
    "competitive_analysis": {
      "description": "Research multiple competitors or comparable products in parallel, reconcile the findings along common dimensions, and produce a competitive intelligence brief with a comparison table and visual summary. Example triggers: 'compare ProductA, ProductB, ProductC on pricing and features', 'Wettbewerbsanalyse für drei SaaS-Produkte', 'run competitive research across these three vendors', 'Vergleichsanalyse mit Tabelle und Differenzierungsmerkmalen', 'competitive snapshot for the payments space', 'Wettbewerbsübersicht mit Visual Brief'.",
      "task": "Use mission_coordinator to partition {{competitors}} into per-competitor research tasks and fan them out in parallel. For each competitor, use researcher to collect public evidence (pricing pages, product docs, case studies, press coverage) and research_librarian to persist notes keyed by competitor. Use evidence_analyst to normalise the findings across {{dimensions}} so every competitor is scored on the same axes. Use data_analyst when any dimension is numeric. Use chart_designer to produce a comparison table (and a differentiator-highlight chart when the numeric evidence is strong enough). Use paper_author or summarizer to write the final {{deliverable}} for {{audience}}, leading with a one-paragraph verdict and the comparison table. Run exactly one quality_supervisor pass before finalising. Do not invent pricing or features that are not backed by a cited source.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "mission_coordinator",
        "researcher",
        "research_librarian",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "data_analyst",
        "chart_designer",
        "paper_author",
        "summarizer",
        "quality_supervisor",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "competitors": {
          "description": "Comma-separated competitor names or products",
          "default": "the competitors named in the user request"
        },
        "dimensions": {
          "description": "Comparison dimensions (e.g. pricing, features, market-position)",
          "default": "pricing, features, market-position"
        },
        "deliverable": {
          "description": "Preferred final deliverable",
          "default": "competitive intelligence brief with comparison table and key differentiators"
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience",
          "default": "product and strategy stakeholders"
        }
      }
    },
    "data_pipeline_review": {
      "description": "Inspect a data source — database, file, or pipeline — for schema quality, null rates, anomalies, and referential integrity; produce a structured data quality report. Example triggers: 'run a data quality check on the orders table', 'Schema-Prüfung für die Kundendatei', 'check null rates and duplicates in the CSV export', 'Datenqualitätsanalyse mit Anomalieerkennung', 'review the pipeline output for integrity issues', 'Datenqualitätsbericht mit Issue-Tabelle'.",
      "task": "Use document_intake when the data source is a file attachment and needs to be read in. Use sql_specialist when the source is a database or table — start by inspecting the schema (columns, types, nullability, keys, indexes) with read-only queries, then run the checks listed in {{validationRules}}: null/empty rates per column, type consistency (bad casts, mixed types), referential integrity (orphaned foreign keys), row-count drift versus prior snapshot if available, duplicate keys, and value distributions (outliers, unexpected enums). Use data_analyst to compute distribution summaries and flag anomalies. Use evidence_analyst to reconcile findings into a single issue ledger: severity, column, rule, sample offending rows (redact PII), recommended action. Use chart_designer when a distribution or trend chart materially helps. Use summarizer to produce the final {{outputFormat}} led by a one-paragraph verdict and an issue table. qa_guard verifies that every issue in the report is traceable to a real query result. Do not perform data mutations from this scene — reporting only.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "sql_specialist",
        "data_analyst",
        "document_intake",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "chart_designer",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "dataSource": {
          "description": "File path, database name, table, or description of the data source",
          "default": "attached files or configured database"
        },
        "validationRules": {
          "description": "Comma-separated validation rules to enforce",
          "default": "null rates, type consistency, referential integrity, row-count drift, value distributions, duplicate keys"
        },
        "outputFormat": {
          "description": "Final report format",
          "default": "markdown data quality report with issue table"
        }
      }
    },
    "content_creation": {
      "description": "Research a topic (when needed), produce audience-ready written content in a specified format and tone, and hand off a finished draft ready for review or publication. Example triggers: 'write a blog post about our new feature', 'Blog-Artikel zum neuen Release verfassen', 'draft a product newsletter for this month', 'Content-Entwurf für die Landingpage', 'produce documentation for the new endpoint', 'Fachartikel mit Recherche und Korrekturlesen'.",
      "task": "When {{requiresResearch}} indicates research is needed, use researcher to gather supporting evidence first and research_librarian to persist notes keyed to the topic; use evidence_analyst to reconcile claims before drafting. Use content_writer to produce the {{contentType}} for {{audience}} in a {{tone}} voice — lead with the hook, no marketing filler. Use qa_guard to verify claims against the evidence ledger and flag unsourced statements. Run one quality_supervisor pass for structure, voice, and audience-fit before finalising. Use summarizer only when a short summary or social-post variant of the main piece is requested. Do not publish or broadcast from this scene — the deliverable is the draft itself.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "content_writer",
        "researcher",
        "research_librarian",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard",
        "quality_supervisor"
      ],
      "params": {
        "contentType": {
          "description": "blog-post, newsletter, product-copy, documentation, press-release",
          "default": "blog-post"
        },
        "topic": {
          "description": "Topic, angle, or key message for the content",
          "default": "the requested topic"
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Intended audience",
          "default": "general technical audience"
        },
        "tone": {
          "description": "Voice and tone (professional, friendly, technical, playful, urgent)",
          "default": "professional and engaging"
        },
        "requiresResearch": {
          "description": "Whether a research pass is required before drafting (yes/no)",
          "default": "yes"
        }
      }
    },
    "onboarding_packet": {
      "description": "Build a structured onboarding packet for a new team member, role, or project — gathering the relevant resources, translating to the target language when needed, and producing a ready-to-share document with a quick-reference table. Example triggers: 'create an onboarding packet for a new backend engineer', 'Einarbeitungsunterlagen für neuen Projektmanager', 'assemble an onboarding document for the StarlingAI platform', 'Onboarding-Paket mit Quick-Reference erstellen', 'put together an intro guide for the new PM', 'Willkommensdokument mit Ressourcenübersicht'.",
      "task": "Use document_intake when existing onboarding material or attachments are provided. Use researcher to fill identifiable gaps (links to docs, tools, access request processes, team rituals, glossary entries) for {{topic}} — do not invent processes that are not actually documented. Use content_writer to assemble the packet for {{audience}}, structured as: Welcome + role context → First-week priorities → Access and accounts checklist → Key contacts → Architecture or product overview → Glossary → Quick-reference table (tool / purpose / link / owner). Use translator to render the final packet in {{outputLanguage}} when it is not English. Use meeting_briefing_agent when the audience expects a first-meeting handoff section with agenda points. Use qa_guard to verify every referenced link and contact against the gathered evidence — do not ship broken references. Produce {{outputFormat}}.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "document_intake",
        "researcher",
        "research_librarian",
        "content_writer",
        "translator",
        "meeting_briefing_agent",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "topic": {
          "description": "What the person is being onboarded to (platform, project, role)",
          "default": "the requested onboarding topic"
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Who is being onboarded and their background",
          "default": "new team member with intermediate technical background"
        },
        "outputLanguage": {
          "description": "Language for the final packet",
          "default": "English"
        },
        "outputFormat": {
          "description": "Packet format",
          "default": "structured Markdown with sections and a quick-reference table"
        }
      }
    },
    "software_bug_fix": {
      "description": "Diagnose a reported bug against the current source tree, apply the smallest viable fix, verify with tests, optionally open a GitHub PR with the change, and hand back a clean change summary. Example triggers: 'fix the null-pointer crash in the order service and open a PR', 'behebe den Fehler im Login-Flow und eröffne einen PR', 'diagnose and patch the rendering bug on the dashboard', 'Bug im Zahlungs-Handler reparieren', 'apply a minimal fix for the flaky queue consumer', 'Softwarefehler untersuchen, beheben und PR erstellen'.",
      "task": "Use git_developer to capture the current branch state and any recent commits that touched the suspected area. Use code_analyst to diagnose the bug described by {{bugReport}} — start from the exact error or symptom, read only the code paths involved, and identify the single smallest change that fixes the root cause. Use coder to apply that minimal fix; do not refactor surrounding code. Use test_generator to add a regression test covering the bug (or amend the existing one) and to run the relevant test subset. If tests fail, perform at most one more fix-test cycle ({{maxCycles}} total), then stop and report. Use qa_guard to verify the fix actually addresses the reported symptom, and summarizer to produce the final change summary: what was broken, why, the exact diff (file + function), the new test, and any remaining risk. When {{openPr}} is yes, git_developer should commit the fix on a fresh branch (e.g. fix/<short-bug-slug>), git_push the branch, and call github_pr_create with title, the change-summary as body (Markdown), head=that branch, base={{prBaseBranch}}; this is approval-gated. Do not rename, extract helpers, or stylistic clean-ups unless required by the fix.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "git_developer",
        "code_analyst",
        "coder",
        "test_generator",
        "qa_guard",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "humanInLoopSteps": [
        "git_push",
        "github_pr_create"
      ],
      "approvalChannel": "slack-approvals",
      "params": {
        "bugReport": {
          "description": "Description of the bug, failing behaviour, error message, or repro steps",
          "default": "the reported bug"
        },
        "targetRef": {
          "description": "Git ref or branch to apply the fix on",
          "default": "current branch"
        },
        "maxCycles": {
          "description": "Maximum fix-test iterations (hard ceiling)",
          "default": "2"
        },
        "openPr": {
          "description": "Whether to push the fix branch and open a GitHub PR after the fix is verified",
          "default": "no"
        },
        "prBaseBranch": {
          "description": "Target branch for the PR when openPr=yes",
          "default": "main"
        }
      }
    },
    "accessibility_audit": {
      "description": "Run a WCAG 2.2 accessibility audit against a live web page or component, capture browser evidence, and produce a structured issue report with severity, guideline reference, and remediation. Example triggers: 'run an accessibility audit on the landing page', 'WCAG-Prüfung für die Login-Seite durchführen', 'check the checkout flow for a11y issues', 'Barrierefreiheit der Anwendung prüfen', 'audit the dashboard against WCAG 2.2', 'Accessibility-Report für das Webportal erstellen'.",
      "task": "Use accessibility_tester to drive the audit. For dynamic pages, use browser_agent to open {{targetUrl}}, wait for the relevant state, and capture browser_snapshot evidence plus computed-style/DOM info needed for the checks. Run the WCAG {{wcagLevel}} checks covering: text alternatives, contrast, keyboard operability, focus management, semantic structure/headings, form labelling, ARIA correctness, time-based media alternatives, and motion/animation safety. Use vision_browser_analyst when a screenshot-based check is needed (e.g. contrast, layout). Use evidence_analyst to reconcile findings into one issue ledger keyed by WCAG success criterion — severity (critical/serious/moderate/minor), guideline reference (e.g. 1.4.3), element selector, failing evidence (quoted DOM or computed style), and a specific remediation. Use qa_guard to verify every listed issue is backed by real captured evidence (no speculative claims). Use summarizer to produce the final {{outputFormat}} led by a one-paragraph verdict and the issue table grouped by severity.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "accessibility_tester",
        "browser_agent",
        "vision_browser_analyst",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "humanInLoopSteps": [
        "site_fill_credentials"
      ],
      "params": {
        "targetUrl": {
          "description": "Page or component URL to audit",
          "default": ""
        },
        "wcagLevel": {
          "description": "WCAG conformance level to test against (A, AA, AAA)",
          "default": "AA"
        },
        "outputFormat": {
          "description": "Final report format",
          "default": "markdown report with severity-grouped issue table and remediation column"
        }
      }
    },
    "translation_task": {
      "description": "Translate a document, message, or attached text between languages, preserving meaning, tone, and formatting, with an optional QA spot-check for critical content. Example triggers: 'translate this announcement to German', 'übersetze das Dokument ins Englische', 'localise these UI strings to French', 'Fachtext ins Deutsche übersetzen', 'translate the attached contract to Spanish', 'Übersetzung mit Qualitätsprüfung anfertigen'.",
      "task": "Use document_intake when the source is an attached file and needs to be read first. Use translator to render the source into {{targetLanguage}} from {{sourceLanguage}} (auto-detect when unspecified), preserving formatting (Markdown, lists, code blocks, placeholders like {{variable}} or $t('key')), tone ({{tone}}), and domain-specific terminology. Do not reinterpret the content — translate what is there. Keep proper names, product names, code identifiers, and URLs unchanged. When {{qaCheck}} is yes, use qa_guard to spot-check critical sentences for meaning preservation, and re-translate only the flagged segments. Use summarizer only when the caller explicitly asks for a bilingual summary next to the translation. Return the translation as the primary deliverable; include a brief translator's note only when ambiguous source text forced a judgement call.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "document_intake",
        "translator",
        "qa_guard",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "params": {
        "targetLanguage": {
          "description": "Language to translate into (ISO name or code)",
          "default": "English"
        },
        "sourceLanguage": {
          "description": "Source language (or 'auto' to detect)",
          "default": "auto"
        },
        "tone": {
          "description": "Preferred tone (formal, neutral, casual, technical)",
          "default": "preserve source tone"
        },
        "qaCheck": {
          "description": "Whether to run a QA spot-check on critical sentences (yes/no)",
          "default": "no"
        }
      }
    },
    "infrastructure_change": {
      "description": "Plan AND APPLY an infrastructure change with explicit human approval (Terraform apply, Ansible playbook run, Kubernetes rollout, kubectl apply, helm upgrade, ssh deployment). Example triggers (must contain an action verb): 'apply the Terraform change to scale the cluster', 'Infrastruktur-\u00c4nderung mit Genehmigung ausrollen', 'run the Ansible playbook to rotate credentials', 'Kubernetes-Rollout starten', 'provision the new VPC with approval', 'Infrastruktur\u00e4nderung umsetzen'.",
      "triggers": {
        "requiresActionVerb": true,
        "patterns": [
          { "all": ["\\b(terraform|ansible|kubectl|helm|kubernetes)\\b", "\\b(apply|deploy|rollout|roll\\s*out|run|provision|scale|migrate|upgrade|install|ausroll(?:en|e)?|anwend(?:en|e)?|umsetz(?:en|e)?|provisionier(?:en|e)?|skalier(?:en|e)?|migrier(?:en|e)?|aktualisier(?:en|e)?|aufsetz(?:en|e)?|durchf(?:u|ue|\u00fc)hr(?:en|e)?)\\b"] },
          { "all": ["\\b(infrastruktur|infrastructure)\\b", "\\b(\\u00e4nderung|aenderung|change|umstellung|migration|rollout)\\b", "\\b(ausroll(?:en|e)?|anwend(?:en|e)?|umsetz(?:en|e)?|durchf(?:u|ue|\u00fc)hr(?:en|e)?|apply|deploy|run|execute)\\b"] }
        ]
      },
      "task": "Use devops_coordinator to direct the flow. First: plan only. Use infrastructure_agent to produce the change plan for {{changeDescription}} against {{targetEnvironment}} — a Terraform plan, Ansible dry-run, or Kubernetes diff — and present the exact set of resources that will be created, modified, or destroyed. Use policy_compliance_reviewer to evaluate the plan against configured policy (tag rules, naming, regions, cost guards, security posture) and flag blockers. PAUSE for operator approval before any apply step — do not execute terraform_exec, ansible_playbook, ansible_task, or destructive ssh_exec operations without approval (these are human-in-loop gated). After approval, use shell_agent or infrastructure_agent to execute the approved plan, capturing the real tool output (not a paraphrase). Use infrastructure_agent to verify the post-change state matches the plan (resource presence, health checks, smoke checks). Use summarizer to produce the final change log: what was planned, what was approved, what actually applied, verification evidence, and rollback notes. If compliance blocks or verification fails, stop and report; do not auto-revert without explicit instruction.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "devops_coordinator",
        "infrastructure_agent",
        "policy_compliance_reviewer",
        "shell_agent",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "humanInLoopSteps": [
        "terraform_exec",
        "ansible_playbook",
        "ansible_task",
        "kubectl_apply",
        "kubectl_delete",
        "kubectl_rollout_restart",
        "kubectl_scale",
        "helm_upgrade",
        "helm_rollback",
        "ssh_exec",
        "service_check"
      ],
      "approvalChannel": "slack-approvals",
      "params": {
        "changeDescription": {
          "description": "The infrastructure change to plan and apply",
          "default": "the requested infrastructure change"
        },
        "targetEnvironment": {
          "description": "Target environment (dev, staging, production)",
          "default": "staging"
        },
        "changeType": {
          "description": "Change tooling (terraform, ansible, kubernetes, shell)",
          "default": "terraform"
        }
      }
    },
    "calendar_scheduling": {
      "description": "Check participant availability, propose or book a calendar event, and send channel confirmations to the invitees. Example triggers: 'schedule a 30-minute sync with the platform team next week', 'buche einen Termin mit dem Kunden für Freitag', 'find a time for a three-person kickoff meeting', 'Meeting für die Projektbesprechung planen', 'book the design review and send invites', 'Kalenderevent mit Bestätigung koordinieren'.",
      "task": "Use calendar_agent to check availability windows for {{participants}} within {{timeRange}} and propose the earliest viable slots (at least two options when possible) matching {{durationMinutes}}. Respect working-hours conventions and timezone differences across participants. Once a slot is selected (or confirmed by the caller), use calendar_agent to create the event with a clear title, description, location/link, and the full participant list. Use mail_agent to send calendar invites when email is the primary confirmation channel, and notification_agent for Slack/Telegram/Discord reminders when {{confirmChannel}} specifies one. Use summarizer to produce a brief confirmation: scheduled date/time (with timezone), participants, join link, and next action. Do not double-book without surfacing the conflict first, and do not create recurring events unless explicitly requested.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "calendar_agent",
        "mail_agent",
        "notification_agent",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "params": {
        "participants": {
          "description": "Comma-separated list of participant emails or names",
          "default": "participants named in the user request"
        },
        "timeRange": {
          "description": "Window to search for available slots",
          "default": "next 5 working days"
        },
        "durationMinutes": {
          "description": "Meeting duration in minutes",
          "default": "30"
        },
        "confirmChannel": {
          "description": "Primary confirmation channel (email, slack, telegram, discord)",
          "default": "email"
        }
      }
    },
    "code_refactor": {
      "description": "Perform a targeted, behaviour-preserving refactor — rename, extract, de-duplicate, narrow types, simplify — verified by existing tests, delivered as a single clean commit, and optionally shipped as a GitHub PR. Example triggers: 'refactor the order-processing module to remove duplication', 'Refactor der Authentifizierungs-Logik', 'extract the shared validation helpers into one module and open a PR', 'Typ-Signaturen im Payment-Service straffen', 'simplify the config loader without behaviour changes', 'Code-Refactoring mit Test-Verifikation und PR'.",      "triggers": {
        "patterns": [
          { "all": ["\\brefactor(?:ing|ed|s)?\\b"] },
          { "all": ["\\b(extract|dedupe|deduplicate|narrow|simplify)\\b", "\\b(function|module|helper|class|method|component|service|type)s?\\b"] }
        ]
      },      "task": "Use git_developer to capture the current branch state. Use code_analyst to scope {{refactorTarget}} to the smallest coherent change — identify exactly the files, functions, and call sites involved, and confirm the refactor is behaviour-preserving (no API change, no logic change). Reject scope creep: do not rename unrelated identifiers, do not rewrite adjacent code. Use coder to apply the refactor in a single pass. Use test_generator to run the existing relevant tests; add tests only when coverage is clearly missing at the refactored boundary. If tests fail, perform at most one more refactor-test cycle ({{maxCycles}} total), then stop and report. Use qa_guard to verify no public API surface changed and no call site was silently broken. Use summarizer to produce the final change summary: what was refactored, the diff scope (file + function count), why it is behaviour-preserving, and the test evidence. When {{openPr}} is yes, git_developer should commit the refactor on a fresh branch (e.g. refactor/<short-target-slug>), git_push the branch, and call github_pr_create with title, the change-summary as Markdown body, head=that branch, base={{prBaseBranch}}; this is approval-gated. Use git_developer only when the caller asks to stage or commit — never push automatically without openPr=yes.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "git_developer",
        "code_analyst",
        "coder",
        "test_generator",
        "qa_guard",
        "summarizer"
      ],
      "humanInLoopSteps": [
        "git_push",
        "github_pr_create"
      ],
      "approvalChannel": "slack-approvals",
      "params": {
        "refactorTarget": {
          "description": "Module, file, or concern to refactor",
          "default": "the requested refactor target"
        },
        "refactorType": {
          "description": "Kind of refactor (rename, extract, dedupe, narrow-types, simplify)",
          "default": "simplify"
        },
        "openPr": {
          "description": "Whether to push the refactor branch and open a GitHub PR after verification",
          "default": "no"
        },
        "prBaseBranch": {
          "description": "Target branch for the PR when openPr=yes",
          "default": "main"
        },
        "maxCycles": {
          "description": "Maximum refactor-test iterations (hard ceiling)",
          "default": "2"
        }
      }
    },
    "database_migration": {
      "description": "Inspect the current schema, author an idempotent migration, run a compliance review, apply it only after explicit human approval, and verify the resulting schema and data. Example triggers: 'add a not-null email column to the users table', 'Datenbankmigration für den neuen Index vorbereiten', 'create the orders_audit table with foreign keys', 'Schema-Migration mit Genehmigung ausrollen', 'add an index on payments.status', 'Datenbankänderung planen und anwenden'.",      "triggers": {
        "requiresActionVerb": true,
        "patterns": [
          { "all": ["\\b(schema|datenbank|database|migration|tabelle|table|spalte|column|index|constraint|foreign\\s*key)\\b", "\\b(add|create|alter|drop|backfill|migrate|hinzuf(?:u|ue|\u00fc)g(?:en|e)?|erstell(?:en|e)?|migrier(?:en|e)?|anwend(?:en|e)?|ausroll(?:en|e)?|durchf(?:u|ue|\u00fc)hr(?:en|e)?)\\b"] }
        ]
      },      "task": "Use sql_specialist in read-only mode first. Inspect the current schema for {{targetDatabase}} — columns, types, nullability, keys, indexes, and any existing migration history. Use code_analyst when the change requires code-side updates (ORM model, type definitions, query callers) so those are enumerated up front. Author the migration as an idempotent, reversible script (use IF NOT EXISTS / IF EXISTS guards, explicit UP and DOWN). For non-trivial changes, include backfill and constraint-enablement as separate phased steps (add nullable → backfill → enforce NOT NULL) so large tables do not block. Use policy_compliance_reviewer against configured data-handling rules (PII, retention, naming). PAUSE for operator approval before any write — do not execute the migration without explicit approval (sql_query write path is human-in-loop gated). After approval, execute the migration in {{targetEnvironment}} and verify: schema matches expectation, no unintended rows changed, indexes present, backfill complete. Use test_generator to run post-migration smoke tests when available. Use summarizer to produce the final migration log: planned change, approved change, applied change, verification evidence, rollback script. Do not auto-rollback without explicit instruction.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "sql_specialist",
        "code_analyst",
        "policy_compliance_reviewer",
        "test_generator",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "humanInLoopSteps": [
        "sql_query"
      ],
      "approvalChannel": "slack-approvals",
      "params": {
        "migrationDescription": {
          "description": "The schema or data migration to plan and apply",
          "default": "the requested migration"
        },
        "targetDatabase": {
          "description": "Database name or connection label",
          "default": "configured primary database"
        },
        "targetEnvironment": {
          "description": "Environment to apply against (dev, staging, production)",
          "default": "staging"
        }
      }
    },
    "contract_review": {
      "description": "Review a contract, NDA, SOW, MSA, or similar legal document — build a clause inventory, produce a risk table, and surface negotiation points. Legal analysis only; the output is advisory and requires human review before any action. Example triggers: 'review this vendor MSA for risky clauses', 'Vertrag auf Risiken prüfen', 'analyse the NDA and list negotiation points', 'Vertragsprüfung mit Risiko-Tabelle erstellen', 'flag any unusual indemnity or liability terms in this SOW', 'juristische Dokumentenprüfung mit Handlungsempfehlungen'.",
      "task": "Use document_intake to load {{contractSource}} and extract clean text (preserve clause numbering where present). Use contract_analyst to build the clause inventory: governing law, term/termination, payment and fees, SLAs, liability cap, indemnity, IP assignment, confidentiality, data protection / GDPR, subprocessors, audit rights, warranties, force majeure, assignment, and {{jurisdictionFocus}}-specific considerations. Score each clause for risk against {{riskAppetite}} (low/medium/high tolerance) and flag non-standard or missing clauses. Use policy_compliance_reviewer to map clauses to configured internal compliance requirements (data handling, security, procurement rules). Use evidence_analyst to reconcile findings into one ledger keyed by clause: clause reference, summary, risk rating, rationale (quoting the contract text verbatim), and suggested negotiation redline. Use qa_guard to verify every risk claim quotes the actual contract text rather than paraphrasing. Use summarizer to produce the final {{outputFormat}}: one-paragraph verdict, risk table (clause / rating / issue / proposed redline), and a prioritised negotiation-points list. State explicitly that this is legal analysis assistance — final legal approval requires human counsel.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "document_intake",
        "contract_analyst",
        "policy_compliance_reviewer",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "params": {
        "contractSource": {
          "description": "Path to the contract file or description of the document",
          "default": "the attached contract"
        },
        "contractType": {
          "description": "Kind of contract (MSA, SOW, NDA, DPA, EULA, employment, vendor)",
          "default": "MSA"
        },
        "jurisdictionFocus": {
          "description": "Primary jurisdiction focus for the review",
          "default": "EU and Germany"
        },
        "riskAppetite": {
          "description": "Risk tolerance (low, medium, high)",
          "default": "medium"
        },
        "outputFormat": {
          "description": "Final report format",
          "default": "markdown report with risk table and negotiation-points list"
        }
      }
    },
    "monitoring_setup": {
      "description": "Define or update alert rules, thresholds, and dashboards for a service or system, review for noise and coverage, apply only after explicit human approval, and verify the monitors are live. Example triggers: 'set up monitoring for the payments service', 'Alerts für den neuen Dienst konfigurieren', 'add a latency-spike alert on the API gateway', 'Dashboard für Fehlerquoten einrichten', 'tighten the CPU threshold on the workers', 'Monitoring-Konfiguration mit Freigabe ausrollen'.",
      "task": "Use devops_coordinator to direct the flow. Use infrastructure_agent to inventory existing monitors, alert rules, and dashboards for {{targetService}} in {{monitoringStack}} — prometheus_query for current metric coverage, alertmanager_silences_list for active mutes, kubectl_get on PrometheusRule resources for rule inventory, and grafana_dashboard_search for dashboard coverage. Author the rule changes for {{signalType}} — alert name, signal/metric expression, thresholds ({{thresholds}}), evaluation window, severity, notification channel, and a clear runbook link. Each rule must have an explicit non-flapping window and a described remediation path; reject anything that would page on normal-operation noise. Use policy_compliance_reviewer to validate the rules against configured on-call and alerting policy (naming, severity taxonomy, channel routing, silence rules). PAUSE for operator approval before any apply step — Prometheus rules apply via kubectl_apply against PrometheusRule custom resources, Grafana changes via grafana_dashboard_apply / grafana_alert_apply, and any maintenance mute via alertmanager_silence_create (all human-in-loop gated). After approval, verify the monitors are live: prometheus_query the new expressions to confirm they evaluate, grafana_dashboard_search to confirm dashboard visibility, and alertmanager_silences_list to confirm no unintended mutes are in effect. Use summarizer to produce the final change log: planned rules, approved rules, applied rules, verification evidence, and rollback steps. Do not disable existing alerts without calling them out explicitly in the approval step.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "devops_coordinator",
        "infrastructure_agent",
        "policy_compliance_reviewer",
        "shell_agent",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard"
      ],
      "humanInLoopSteps": [
        "kubectl_apply",
        "grafana_dashboard_apply",
        "grafana_alert_apply",
        "alertmanager_silence_create",
        "alertmanager_silence_expire"
      ],
      "approvalChannel": "slack-approvals",
      "params": {
        "targetService": {
          "description": "Service, component, or system to monitor",
          "default": "the requested service"
        },
        "monitoringStack": {
          "description": "Monitoring tooling (prometheus, grafana, datadog, cloudwatch, other)",
          "default": "prometheus"
        },
        "signalType": {
          "description": "Signal class (latency, error-rate, saturation, availability, custom)",
          "default": "error-rate and latency"
        },
        "thresholds": {
          "description": "Threshold guidance (e.g. 'p95 latency > 500ms for 5m')",
          "default": "reasonable SLO-aligned defaults — ask for confirmation if unclear"
        }
      }
    },
    "feature_planning": {
      "description": "Turn a product feature request into a structured plan: user stories, acceptance criteria, dependency graph, and a task breakdown, with explicit human approval before locking scope and handing off to execution. Example triggers: 'plan out the multi-tenant billing feature', 'Feature-Planung für den neuen Upload-Flow', 'break this user request into acceptance criteria and tasks', 'Roadmap-Feature in User Stories aufteilen', 'turn the product brief into a task graph', 'Feature-Planung mit Abhängigkeiten und Abnahmekriterien'.",
      "task": "Use researcher when the feature touches an unfamiliar domain or competitive context — collect the minimum evidence needed to ground scope decisions (do not run a full competitive analysis from this scene). Use evidence_analyst to consolidate stated requirements, constraints, and open questions from {{featureBrief}}. Use project_planner to produce: (1) user stories in 'As a <role>, I want <goal>, so that <outcome>' form, (2) Gherkin-style acceptance criteria for each story with explicit edge cases, (3) a dependency graph identifying upstream blockers and cross-team dependencies, (4) a task breakdown sized for {{targetTimeHorizon}} with rough t-shirt estimates, and (5) a milestone plan with entry/exit criteria. Use content_writer to render the deliverable as {{outputFormat}} structured for {{audience}}. End by calling ask_user with a concise plan summary and an explicit approval prompt — do not open tickets, hand off to `project_planner` execution mode, or kick off downstream work without that operator confirmation. Use qa_guard to verify that every acceptance criterion is testable and every task maps to at least one acceptance criterion. Do not invent scope the brief does not support — call out gaps explicitly instead.",
      "allowedAgents": [
        "project_planner",
        "researcher",
        "evidence_analyst",
        "content_writer",
        "summarizer",
        "qa_guard",
        "quality_supervisor"
      ],
      "params": {
        "featureBrief": {
          "description": "Product feature description, user request, or problem statement",
          "default": "the requested feature"
        },
        "audience": {
          "description": "Who the plan is written for (engineering, product, cross-functional)",
          "default": "cross-functional team"
        },
        "targetTimeHorizon": {
          "description": "Planning horizon (one-sprint, one-quarter, major-initiative)",
          "default": "one-sprint"
        },
        "outputFormat": {
          "description": "Final deliverable format",
          "default": "structured Markdown plan with user stories, acceptance criteria, dependency graph, and milestone list"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
